{"id":"scaled-dot-product-attention","t":"Scaled dot-product attention with a mask","tags":["Attention","Transformers"],"d":"Medium","rt":"numpy","fn":"attention","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"Attention lets each query mix the values whose keys it matches best:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \\mathrm{softmax}\\!\\left(\\frac{QK^\\top}{\\sqrt{d_k}} + M\\right) V$$\n\nWrite `attention(Q, K, V, mask=None)`.\n\n## Inputs\n- `Q`: `(n, d_k)` queries, `K`: `(m, d_k)` keys, `V`: `(m, d_v)` values.\n- `mask`: optional boolean `(n, m)`. `True` means query $i$ **may** attend to key $j$. Masked positions get $-\\infty$ before the softmax.\n\n## Output\n- `(n, d_v)` array.\n- If a query row has **no** allowed keys, its output row is all zeros (not `nan`).","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef attention(Q, K, V, mask=None):\n    \"\"\"Q: (n, dk), K: (m, dk), V: (m, dv), mask: bool (n, m) or None. Return (n, dv).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Build the `(n, m)` score matrix with one product, `Q @ K.T`, then divide by `sqrt(d_k)`.","Apply the mask with `np.where(mask, scores, -np.inf)`, then take a stable softmax per row. `exp(-inf)` is exactly 0, so masked keys get zero weight.","A row that is entirely `-inf` has max `-inf`, and `-inf - -inf` is `nan`. Replace a non-finite row max with 0, and guard the division so all-zero rows stay zero."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef attention(Q, K, V, mask=None):\n    Q, K, V = (np.asarray(x, dtype=float) for x in (Q, K, V))\n    scores = Q @ K.T / np.sqrt(Q.shape[-1])\n    if mask is not None:\n        scores = np.where(mask, scores, -np.inf)\n    row_max = scores.max(axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n    row_max = np.where(np.isfinite(row_max), row_max, 0.0)     # fully masked row: avoid -inf - -inf\n    w = np.exp(scores - row_max)                               # exp(-inf) = 0 for masked keys\n    total = w.sum(axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n    w = np.divide(w, total, out=np.zeros_like(w), where=total > 0)\n    return w @ V\n","ex_md":"## Why divide by $\\sqrt{d_k}$\nIf the entries of $q$ and $k$ are independent with unit variance, $q\\cdot k$ has variance $d_k$. Without scaling, scores grow with the head size, softmax saturates toward one-hot, and its gradient vanishes. Dividing by $\\sqrt{d_k}$ brings the variance back to 1.\n\n## Masks\n- **Causal** (decoder LMs): query $i$ sees keys $j \\le i$. That's `np.tril`, test 3.\n- **Padding**: hide padded keys so real tokens never attend to them.\nSetting scores to $-\\infty$ (not just a large negative number) guarantees exactly zero weight. You then have to handle the fully masked row yourself, and that is the bug interviewers probe.\n\n## Complexity\n$O(n m d_k)$ for scores and $O(n m d_v)$ for the mix. The $(n, m)$ matrix is the memory problem for long contexts, and FlashAttention exists to avoid ever materialising it.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Extend to multi-head: reshape to `(h, n, d_k/h)`, attend per head, concatenate, project.\n- Why does a KV cache make decoding $O(m)$ per new token instead of $O(m^2)$?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[1., 0.]])\narray([[1., 0.],\n       [0., 1.]])\narray([[10.],\n       [20.]])","out":"array([[13.30238451]])"},{"in":"array([[ 0.30471708, -1.03998411,  0.7504512 ,  0.94056472, -1.95103519,\n        -1.30217951,  0.1278404 , -0.31624259],\n       [-0.01680116, -0.85304393,  0.87939797,  0.77779194,  0.0660307 ,\n         1.12724121,  0.46750934, -0.85929246],\n       [ 0.36875078, -0.9588826 ,  0.8784503 , -0.04992591, -0.18486236,\n        -0.68092954,  1.22254134, -0.15452948],\n       [-0.42832782, -0.35213355,  0.53230919,  0.36544406,  0.41273261,\n         0.430821  ,  2.1416476 , -0.40641502]])\narray([[-0.51224273, -0.81377273,  0.61597942,  1.12897229, -0.11394746,\n        -0.84015648, -0.82448122,  0.65059279],\n       [ 0.74325417,  0.54315427, -0.66550971,  0.23216132,  0.11668581,\n         0.2186886 ,  0.87142878,  0.22359555],\n       [ 0.67891356,  0.06757907,  0.2891194 , 